As one of the largest data science research incubator initiatives in the country, the
University of California, Berkeley's Data Science Discovery Program serves as a case
study for a scalable and sustainable model of data science consulting in higher educa
tion. This case contributes to the broader literature on data science consulting in
higher education by analysing the programme's development, institutional influences;
staffing and structural model; and defining features, which may prove instructive to
similar programmes at other institutions. The programme is characterised by a unique
structure of undergraduate consultations led by graduate student mentorship and
governance; a streamlined, multidepartmental model that facilitates scalability and
sustainability; and diverse modes for undergraduate consulting—including one
on-one ad-hoc data science consultations, extended data science project develop
ment and management, peer mentorship and data science workshop instruction. This
case demonstrates that universities may be able to initiate a low-stakes, small-scale
data science consulting initiative and then progressively scale up the project in collab
oration with multiple departments and organisations across campus.
KEYWORDS
consulting, data science, data science studies, higher education, statistical consulting
1 | INTRODUCTION
The case of the University of California, Berkeley's Data Science Discovery Program (DS Discovery) offers an important contribution to our under
standing and practice of data science consulting. Primarily staffed by undergraduate students, DS Discovery offers twofold peer consulting ser
vices to the broader UC Berkeley undergraduate community and extended consulting engagement to projects within DS Discovery. DS Discovery
is characterised by a unique structure of undergraduate consultations led by graduate student mentorship and governance; a streamlined, multi
departmental model that facilitates scalability and sustainability; and diverse modes for undergraduate consulting—including one-on-one ad-hoc
data science consultations, extended data science project development and management, peer mentorship and data science workshop instruction.
Significantly, this case demonstrates that universities may be able to initiate a low-stakes, small-scale data science consulting initiative and then
progressively scale up the project in collaboration with multiple departments and organisations across campus. To the authors' knowledge, these
characteristics have yet to be discussed in the literature and offer valuable contributions to our understanding of data science consulting in higher
education broadly, as well as multidepartmental collaborations and the professional development of undergraduate and graduate students.
DS Discovery Consulting (originally called Data Peer Consulting) started as a campus-wide data science consultation but is now part of the
DS Discovery Program, one of the largest data science research incubator initiatives in the country. The DS Discovery Consulting simultaneously
offers data science consulting services to undergraduate students across campus while also providing extended consulting services to each of the
roughly 400 undergraduate students involved with an average of 100 DS Discovery projects each semester.
