The wider DS Discovery Program connects undergraduate and graduate students at UC Berkeley with researchers in academia, government,
nonprofit and industry to offer students high public-impact data science research and learning opportunities. These projects range from a single
semester to multiyear engagements, and its project partners have included the United Nations, NASA, the American Heart Association, the Bay
Area Rapid Transit, Microsoft, IBM and more in 1000 individual projects since 2015. In addition to providing a unique learning experience for stu
dents, DS Discovery is a professional development opportunity with potential employers. These aspects of DS Discovery draw parallels with
student-led experiential learning programmes in other disciplines (Kaseda et al., 2022; Pomann et al., 2022).
We see the wider contributions from the case of DS Discovery in terms of its structural scalability and sustainability. As an organisation that
has organically developed through partnerships between multiple university departments, its structure and practice are deeply collaborative
across campus, contributing to a wider umbrella of data science consultation and instruction. Moreover, growing out of a philosophy of peer men
torship, DS Discovery is largely staffed and organised by highly engaged undergraduate students mentored by graduate students, further embody
ing a culture of peer mentorship and learning. As an alternative to teaching through simulated experience (Lasser et al., 2021; Shilane et al., 2022),
the programme teaches through direct experience (Barman et al., 2022). In turn, the programme prioritises undergraduate and graduate student
staff's professional development alongside its consulting services. In effect, a sustainable cycle of peer mentorship has been developed where
more experienced Discovery Consultants iteratively train a new pool of consultants to take their place when they graduate. Graduate students
facilitate this process through the providing governance of the consulting programme while also mentoring the undergraduate Discovery
Consultants.
This case is in conversation with previous reflections on how to foster diversity and inclusion in data science (Geiger et al., 2019) and how to
do data-intensive research in teams (Geiger et al., 2018; Sholler et al., 2019). DS Discovery has rapidly scaled since its inception in 2015 and con
stantly reflects on how to promote more inclusive data science both internally and externally. We view its embedded structures of scalability, sus
tainability and equity as pivotal to its successful growth. Like statistical consulting, DS Discovery's data science consultants face a wide array of
research questions from students with varying degrees of experience in data science (Kirk, 1991). Discovery Consultants have a wide range
of data science skill sets, including applied math, geospatial information systems, business economics and cognitive science, equipping the team
with the relevant context to help develop interdisciplinary, socially responsible and effective data science solutions. Moreover, the diversity of
Discovery Consultants extends past their technical expertise, further facilitating mentorship among students with marginalised backgrounds.
We turn now to a brief case description of DS Discovery's development and structure and then close with a discussion of the wider implica
tions of this case.
2 | DEVELOPMENT AND IMPACTS OF INSTITUTIONAL CULTURE
DS Discovery was founded in 2015 as part of the Berkeley Institute for Data Science, an interdisciplinary research hub at the University of
California, Berkeley, that supports data science research, training and open-source software development. In 2018, DS Discovery received
funding from the Schmidt Futures, a philanthropic initiative, which allowed the programme to continue to scale its programming and hire graduate
fellows.
In 2017, the Division of Data Science and the UC Berkeley Library (the Library) established a joint ‘Data Peers’ programme within the newly
remodelled Moffitt Library. Data Peers offered in-person consultations for campus-wide data science consulting needs staffed by upper-level
undergraduate students who were initially all volunteers. The Data Peers programme then developed in partnerships with other departments on
campus such as the Social Science Data Laboratory (D-Lab) and Research Information Technology (Research IT). These essential partnerships have
further developed since 2017 to a point where current Discovery Consultants are often co-located between organisations. For example, a Discov
ery Consultant might co-consult on data science projects and co-instruct data science workshops with staff at the D-Lab and hold officer hours
with Research IT. In 2019, DS Discovery Consulting received funding from the campus Student Technology Fund to scale its staffing of under
graduate consultants.
With the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, the programme pivoted to entirely virtual consultations and instruction. It leveraged its
existing partnership with the D-Lab to utilise D-Lab's virtual ‘front desk’ hosted on Zoom. D-Lab's front desk is structured in a way where under
graduate students staff the ‘lobby’ of the front desk on Zoom and then direct consultees to breakout rooms to meet with consultants. D-Lab also
manages a large staff of graduate student consultants and full-time staff to maintain the ‘front desk’.
DS Discovery also launched its Data Science Discovery Symposium during this time, which enables students and projects to prepare poster
presentations of their work. The Symposium serves as an opportunity for students to showcase their learning and accomplishments while net
working with potential employers, and Discovery Consultants are often asked to consult on the development of these posters and presentations.
In this period, the DS Discovery Program and D-Lab also launched its Graduate Discovery Fellows programme. This programme funds
Berkeley graduate students to serve as mentors, consultants, advisors and instructors for DS Discovery. These Graduate Discovery Fellows pro
vide mentorship to the team of undergraduate Discovery Consultants, offer consulting services and advise on programme strategy.
