The DS Discovery's Consulting Services also embody the organic, collaborative culture rooted in the programme's development. As a service,
DS Discovery's consulting aims to develop data science capabilities among undergraduates by solving technical issues while promoting student
learning and skills development.
3 | STAFFING AND STRUCTURAL MODEL
The DS Discovery Program is organised between three teams—consulting, outreach and analytics—all staffed by undergraduate students, gradu
ate students and full-time staff. The consulting team has a unique staffing model among these three teams where a team of roughly 10 undergrad
uate students serve as Discovery Consultants and two graduate students serve as Graduate Discovery Fellows. While the two full-time
professional staff are still involved with the consulting team, most of the administration on the consulting side of the programme is handled by
the undergraduate ‘Team Leads’, with the Graduate Discovery Fellows providing mentorship and oversight. Figure 1 illustrates this model.
One of the most innovative aspects of DS Discovery is its unique involvement of undergraduate students as Discovery Consultants. While Dis
covery Consultants leverage their technical knowledge and data science domain emphasis to assist with various data science projects, they also learn
from the exposure to those diverse projects. Unlike established professionals and graduate students, undergraduate Discovery Consultants may be in
the process of developing a more refined outline of their future career goals and long-term professional aspirations. As such, this model helps under
graduate staff expand their toolbelt by learning and applying technical skills within and beyond their domain emphasis, thus becoming better
equipped to pursue compelling career opportunities. DS Discovery does offer not only external assistance to DS Discovery projects and the broader
UC Berkeley undergraduate community but also internal guidance for consultants. One outcome of this model is that the programme has developed
a unique sustainability by cultivating a particularly passionate team of consultants who personally benefit from their work.
Growing out of the Data Peers programme, the DS Discovery Consultant teams are intentionally a student-led effort. Until recently, the under
graduate Discovery Consultants self-organised their consulting efforts, where more experienced consultants were appointed as ‘Team Leads’ who
would help manage newer consultants, lead onboarding and orientation and develop and deliver instructional material. As the programme has scaled,
some of the Graduate Discovery Fellows have provided more strategic guidance on programme planning outside the advanced consulting work.
However, there remains a strong culture of undergraduate initiative and self-organisation. Discovery Consultants self-delegate to complete the tasks
with advising from the graduate fellows. When each of the roughly 100 DS Discovery projects needs to be assigned each semester, the Discovery
Consultants organise among themselves to select their projects. Additionally, instead of assigning a particular data science workshop to a Discovery
Consultant, they develop instructional materials themselves based on their interests, strengths and peer demand.
The consulting team's organic, student-led organisational structure typically begins with an assessment of new consultants' current data science
competencies through the hiring and onboarding process. Discovery Consultants are selected for their advanced skill sets, on-the-job training and
mentorship experience. From there, every Discovery Consultant develops skills related to project management, problem-solving, customer service,
