consultations in their concentration. When new Discovery Consultants join the team, they work closely with Team Leads to develop their expertise
in the specific concentration, positioning them to eventually become Team Leads themselves. This self-sustaining leadership model efficiently trains
and educates new staff, allows experienced Discovery Consultants to practice onboarding and offboarding teammates and guarantees seamless tran
sitions by positioning new Consultants to fill in Team Lead roles in the future.
The workshop development Team Lead collaborates closely with the D-Lab and the Library to organise and conduct workshops on various
data science topics. These workshops cover a spectrum of subjects, including data analysis with Python, text analysis and structured query lan
guage (SQL). By engaging with college students in the Bay Area, both in-person and through virtual sessions on platforms like Zoom, Discovery
Consultants take on leadership roles, guiding participants through the intricacies of these subjects. This not only enhances Discovery Consultants'
teaching and communication skills but also contributes to the data science initiative of making data science education accessible to all by sharing
valuable knowledge with the broader community.
The cloud computing Team Lead works closely with Research IT to support students involved with group projects that leverage cloud computing
resources such as Azure and the Savio High-Performance Cloud Computing service. Through this collaboration, Discovery Consultants not only gain
hands-on experience with cutting-edge technologies but also actively contribute to the successful implementation of data science projects.
Lastly, the project management Team Lead typically takes on projects with more intense management needs, which often entails managing a
project directly. Team Leads in this area receive additional mentorship from the Graduate Discovery Fellows related to the management of teams,
project partner relations and project planning and execution.
4 | IMPLICATIONS FOR DATA SCIENCE CONSULTING
UC Berkeley's Data Science Discovery Program's development and structure reveal many implications for the broader data science
consulting community, its unique structure of undergraduate consultations led by graduate student mentorship and oversight, streamlined, multi
departmental model that has facilitated scalability and sustainability and diversity of modes of undergraduate consulting—including one-on-one
ad-hoc data science consultations, extended data science project development and management, peer mentorship and data science workshop
instruction that are the most salient.
4.1 | Undergraduate and graduate student-led
DS Discovery's model of peer mentoring is a unique contribution to data science consulting. As an alternative to classroom-based experiential
learning or tutoring, the programme offers hands-on, client-facing data science consulting experience for both student researchers and consul
tants, all of whom are at the undergraduate level. Rather than relying solely on experienced professionals and graduate students, this model of
peer mentoring fosters a culture where undergraduate students learn together, reducing some of the barriers of the client-expert relationship.
When an undergraduate Discovery Consultant's abilities are exhausted, they turn to the Graduate Discovery Fellows for advice and potentially
hand off the consultations with the research partner to them. In addition to assisting with technical questions, these Graduate Discovery Fellows
also make strategic decisions on the programme. This model aims at a more sustainable and equitable approach to data science consulting.
This is further seen in its extension through DS Discovery's internal staffing model, which integrates this collaborative, organic culture. Under
graduate consultants and graduate administrators are progressively developed toward greater expertise and responsibility through colearning.
They then go on to train the next generation of staff to take their place as they graduate. In turn, this model has become sustainable in the sense
that it organically reproduces.
4.2 | Collaborative, multidepartmental model
The programme has iteratively developed since the mid-2010s in collaboration with UC Berkeley's Library, Research IT, D-Lab, the Berkeley Insti
tute for Data Science and the College of Computing, Data Science and Society. The highly collaborative origin deeply influences the culture and
