This case demonstrates that universities may be able to initiate a low-stakes, small-scale data science consulting initiative and then progres
sively scale up the project in collaboration with multiple departments and organisations across campus. This cross-campus collaboration may
deepen the organisation's legitimacy, facilitate a sustainable staffing and funding model and support larger university-wide goals.
4.3 | Diversity of modes of consultation
Finally, DS Discovery Consulting has a unique model of a diversity of modes of consultation. Undergraduate Discovery Consultants offer as-need
data science consulting services to UC Berkeley's entire undergraduate community and extended services to an average of 100 data science pro
jects involving over 400 students. The range of consulting services offered through these different mediums includes technical support with
programming languages, methods of data management and analysis, data visualisation, cloud computing and project management that extend to
all phases of the data science process. Moreover, Discovery Consultants develop and lead instructional workshops on various topics in data sci
ence such as programming languages and approaches to machine learning.
This model asks a lot of its student staff. To compensate, staff are supported through a culture and structure of peer mentorship and itera
tively cultivated expertise and responsibility. Consulting questions and administrative issues are shared among the group to reduce potential bur
dens on any one staff member, and staff slowly deepen their expertise as they become Team Leads and take on more leadership roles.
Consequently, the diversity of modes of consultation deepens the staff's expertise and professional competencies.
5 | CONCLUSION
UC Berkeley's Data Science Discovery Program offers a potentially scalable, sustainable and equitable model of data science consulting in higher
education by initiating a low-stakes, small-scale data science consulting initiative with undergraduate and graduate students, progressively scaling
up the project in collaboration with multiple departments and organisations across campus and delivering a diversity of modes of consulting
including one-on-one ad-hoc data science consultations, extended data science project development and management, peer mentorship and data
science workshop instruction. This grassroots, cross-campus collaboration model may deepen the organisation's legitimacy, facilitate a sustainable
staffing and funding model and better support larger university-wide goals. This case contributes to the broader literature on data science consult
ing in higher education by analysing the programme's development, institutional influences; staffing and structural model; and defining features,
which may also prove instructive to similar programmes at other institutions.
